-- title:
--   Unipotence on inertia at a prime exactly dividing the level
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform, i.e. $g$ is a normalised eigenform — its $q$-expansion coefficients satisfy $a_1(g)=1$, $a_{mn}(g)=a_m(g)a_n(g)$ for coprime $m,n$, the recursion $a_{p^{r+2}}(g)=a_p(g)a_{p^{r+1}}(g)-p\,a_{p^r}(g)$ for primes $p\nmid M$ and $a_{p^{r+2}}(g)=a_p(g)a_{p^{r+1}}(g)$ for primes $p\mid M$ — and no proper divisor $M'\mid M$ carries a normalised eigenform of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M')$ whose $\ell$-th coefficients agree with those of $g$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$. Let $\lambda$ be a prime, $S$ a finite set of natural numbers, and $O'$ a characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field such that $\lambda$ lies in its maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(M)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$ and $U_r$ for primes $r\mid M$, $r\notin S$ — to $\mathbb{C}$ with $\chi_g(T_\ell)=a_\ell(g)$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ to $O'$. Let $\rho$ be a [`GaloisRepAdic O'`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16): a monoid homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ (as $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms of `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) to the endomorphisms of a free finite $O'$-module of rank $2$, continuous in the sense that for every $n$ some finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has its pointwise stabiliser acting trivially modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{O'}^n$. Assume that for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $A$ and every $\sigma$ in the decomposition group of $A$ acting on the residue field of $A$ by $x\mapsto x^\ell$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ equals $X^2-\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X+\ell$. Then for every prime $q\neq\lambda$ with $q\mid M$ and $q^2\nmid M$, for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ having $q$ as a nonunit and every $\sigma$ in the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $(X-1)^2$.
--
--   This is the exponent-one clause of Carayol's description of the local behaviour at $q$ of the $\lambda$-adic representation attached to a weight-two newform, in the form asserting unipotence of inertia at a prime $q\neq\lambda$ exactly dividing the level. It feeds the local analysis at primes of multiplicative reduction used in the level-lowering and lifting arguments, and is invoked by the packaging of Hecke–Galois data and by the study of charpolys of inertia for newforms.
